<!--Post: 6136--><!--Time: 1107545110-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST6136"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Iq<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Iq<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Friday, February 04, 2005 - 11:25 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->There is an article on the Wichita Eagle website in which a woman that may have been the original target the day BTK killed Shirley Vian makes a couple of interesting comments. She says that LE asked her if she wore glasses at the time of the murder, she did. <BR> <BR>They also asked if she fequented a bar called &#40;A?&#41; Blackout. She did go there and she speculates that LE was linking the victims to this bar. If this is true it would be the first time I have heard of an actual link between victims. <BR> <BR>Here&#39;s the story:<a href="http://www.kansas.com/mld/kansas/news/special_packages/btk/10813523.htm" target=_top>http://www.kansas.com/mld/kansas/news/special_pack ages/btk/10813523.htm</a> <BR> <BR>iq <BR><!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 6136-->
<!--Post: 6143--><!--Time: 1107550177-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST6143"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Iq<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Iq<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Friday, February 04, 2005 - 12:49 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->The place was called A Blackout. Here is more info on it: <BR><a href="http://www.ku.edu/heritage/beats/ablackout.html" target=_top>http://www.ku.edu/heritage/beats/ablackout.html</a> <BR> <BR>iq<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 6143-->
<!--Post: 6144--><!--Time: 1107550671-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST6144"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Q<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Q<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Friday, February 04, 2005 - 12:57 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->&#39;Happ&#39;y Clown &#40;Pinball Machine&#41; Photo by Tom Bevan <BR> <BR>Skov has links to all this in the Vian Thread.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 6144-->
<!--Post: 6176--><!--Time: 1107565329-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST6176"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Lilymarie<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Lilymarie<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Friday, February 04, 2005 - 5:02 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->A Blackout Tavern. A hangout for all of the &#34;beat&#34; poets of the previous generation - and still a kind of &#34;artsy&#34; meets &#34;biker&#34; hangout in the late 60&#39;s and 70&#39;s ... I&#39;ve posted about it many times here. I thought there might be a connection a couple of months ago when I was a newbie and began with the &#34;Oh Death&#34; folklore, PJ Wyatt theory. My mom went to high school with Bob Dylan so I already have an interest in this subject anyway.  <BR> <BR>Now, I&#39;m going back to some of my original thoughts and going to do more research. Thanks for bringing this back up.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 6176-->
<!--Post: 6178--><!--Time: 1107565667-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST6178"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Lilymarie<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Lilymarie<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Friday, February 04, 2005 - 5:07 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Do some searching on... Ginsberg, Wichita Vortex, Lee Streiff, Beat Poets, Mikrokosmos. It was a very tight clique of &#34;intellectuals&#34; and pot smokers that put Wichita on the map for that generation. Search &#34;A Blackout Tavern&#34; too...very interesting.  <BR> <BR>To put myself out there for ridicule - I wondered if BTK was on the fringe of the group that followed the original Beats &#40;the wannabees&#41; and wasn&#39;t accepted. Maybe PJ Wyatt gave him the impression that he may have some talent with poetry &#40;or he thought she did&#41; but he was rejected by this group.  <BR> <BR>It&#39;s a stretch - but I&#39;ve come down hard on a few of you so...<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 6178-->
<!--Post: 6180--><!--Time: 1107565962-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST6180"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Surfietx<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Surfietx<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->2-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Friday, February 04, 2005 - 5:12 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Lily, see my post in the general section titled T. Bob Kreisch.  <!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 6180-->
<!--Post: 6185--><!--Time: 1107569107-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST6185"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Johno<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Johno<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Friday, February 04, 2005 - 6:05 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->I don&#39;t think that that is a stretch at all. With BTK&#39;s interest in poetry I have often thought a a connection to Ginsberg&#39;s 1966 visit to Wichita, his Wichita Vortex and that whole beat scene era of Wichita. I haven&#39;t yet formulated any connection to it other than BTK&#39;s interest in poetry. He would have most likely been the right age as a young man or teenager to be impressed by it. <BR>Maybe in high school had Lee Streiff as a teacher.  <!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 6185-->
<!--Post: 6218--><!--Time: 1107592001-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST6218"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Pax56<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Pax56<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Saturday, February 05, 2005 - 12:26 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->I like your idea, Lily.  And not to offend any cannabis users in the audience, but heavy use could easily explain BTK&#39;s unusual spelling and grammar &#40;not all users, but I knew some back then who got very sloppy, especially when high&#41;.  Activation of a pre-psychotic condition is also an unfortunate consequence of cannabis for some users.  BTK, ex-pothead?  It would explain a lot.  So many young people went through a big rebellious phase back then - why not BTK?<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 6218-->
<!--Post: 6220--><!--Time: 1107593474-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST6220"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Bmodule<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Bmodule<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->2-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Saturday, February 05, 2005 - 12:51 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Not to mention the availablity of LSD &amp; STP &#40;Exctasy&#41;which might explain a lot of things.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 6220-->
<!--Post: 6289--><!--Time: 1107660517-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST6289"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Bestbud<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Bestbud<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Saturday, February 05, 2005 - 7:28 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Some interesting words &#40;typesetting and railroad ties&#41; and pic of White Van with Logo in the Article: <BR> <BR>Assembled in the similar pursuits of congeniality, the tavern customers, regardless of ideology, fell into a receptive state. In this way, a middle-aged future owner of the bar, Dudley, found himself in proximity to wild youth. &#34;Dudley started coming into the bar during the day. He was a clinical alcoholic who had stayed dry. He&#39;d come in there and do a couple of business deals over the phone—selling typesetting equipment. Then he fell off the wagon.&#34;  <BR> <BR>John Byers ran A Blackout from 1975 to 1978, taking it over from Dudley. According to Byers, Dudley ran a loose ship, sleeping on the bar, giving his dog an inviolable corner, and acting very much annoyed if he were called upon to furnish potations.  <BR> <BR>Byers had definite ideas on tavern management, partly defined through his experiences in the bar when McNabb owned it. &#34;I wanted to bring it back to life. I put seashells everywhere and made it a seaside bar.&#34;  <BR> <BR>He also put wood chips and railroad ties behind the building, as well as a tall fence, so that his patrons could take in the out-of-doors in good weather with some privacy. The entertainment was a jukebox with hard-to-find records whose message was fiercely anti-disco. <BR> <BR><a href="http://www.ku.edu/heritage/beats/ablackout.html" target=_top>http://www.ku.edu/heritage/beats/ablackout.html</a><!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 6289-->
</table></td></tr></table>
